  "2026-10-02T17:09:09.164Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check 2026-10-02: complete strict all-status corpus read returned 2851/2851 items with no omissions or unreadable files; exact GitHub issue/ID and contract-title searches found no canonical owner for this residual acceptance. Existing foundations are linked, including closed historical work; no closed fix is asserted outstanding. Imported report and acceptance are planning evidence, not current reproduction or implementation completion. This item remains open and unclaimed until selected for implementation."
body: "## Goal\n\nMake the default as lightweight as possible while keeping all additional features and customization available, and making the information an agent needs discoverable when needed.\n\nChild/design constraint of #1357. This is an implementation plan, not an implementation PR. #1358–#1362 remain planning work. The core should support an ordinary personal/project task without requiring coding, Git, tests, packages, claims, or advanced context machinery. More capable workflows should opt in without losing safety or information.\n\n## Evidence and limits\n\nAudit date: 2026-09-30. Runtime observations below use published `@unbrained/pm-cli@2026.9.30`; source references are pinned to [main 1e7ecc1](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d).\n\n**Reproduced / inspected**\n- A fresh non-Git tracker can already initialize, create “Buy picnic supplies”, list it, and close it with a reason without packages, claims, or tests. Preserve this working path.\n- Default generated agent guidance still prescribes coding/test operations and orientation plus three pre-mutation reads: [guidance generator](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/src/sdk/init-agent-guidance.ts#L195-L210).\n- Fresh Git initialization installs five merge drivers independently of bundled-package installation and governance preset. The initial install is controlled by `mergeFence`, before the package step: [init](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/src/sdk/init.ts#L1374-L1382). Non-Git init emits a missing-merge-install warning. `--no-merge-fence` skips that initialization step; it is not a persisted disabled capability or an uninstall operation. No public uninstall command was found in the inspected command surface.\n- Health computes `requireMergeDrivers = options.requireMergeDrivers === true || strictExit`: [health](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/src/sdk/governance/health.ts#L3385-L3386). This couples stricter exit behavior to feature requirements.\n- Root help is 3,188 UTF-8 bytes / 42 lines; `create --help` is 8,232 bytes / 106 lines with 83 options. The contracts summary advertises 137 rows and is paginated. These are surface-size measurements, not proof that every option is unnecessary.\n- Existing [token accounting](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/docs/OUTPUT_TOKEN_ACCOUNTING.md) and [release baseline](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/docs/agent-task-token-baseline.json) already measure scripted CLI trajectories. The JSON baseline uses `ceil(utf8_bytes / 4)`, `output_before_token_accounting`, and orientation estimates 1,102 / 2,340 / 5,203. These are estimated emitted-output tokens, not measured live-agent total effort or proof of a net advantage over files. The prose documentation has older example numbers; use the versioned JSON baseline as the numeric source.\n\n**Proposed, not yet implemented**\nA small generic default, explicit persisted capability policy, capability-aware guidance/discovery, and a controlled total-agent-effort comparison. Exact setting/API/command names below are illustrative and must be selected after inventory.\n\n## Existing ownership / deduplication\n\n- #815 is closed and owns the earlier “optional missing merge drivers make health unhealthy” report. Reuse its semantics/tests; this plan adds persisted capability policy and addresses the current strict-exit coupling, rather than refiling that historical bug.\n- #1338 remains the owner for alias/help disagreement and compact-help omission receipts. #1166 is closed; preserve its full-help discovery fix.\n- #443 is closed and covers lean errors. Reuse the implemented error machinery; only change remaining generic-default behavior with new evidence.\n- #863 and [pm-g3n00m](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/.agents/pm/stories/pm-g3n00m.toon) own tokens-per-task accounting; extend through #1362 rather than creating another ledger or evaluation harness.\n- #1358–#1361 supply richer knowledge, coverage, execution and authority contracts. Their capabilities must not become unconditional startup requirements.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n### 1. Define the minimal core and capability contract\n\nInventory current SDK exports, settings, schema/type registries, extensions, governance presets, and initialization/health/validation behavior. Document a capability matrix: always-on core, available opt-in functionality, prerequisites, side effects, persistent state, and discovery path.\n\nKeep basic initialization, create/read/list/search/update/close, required integrity checks, and configured mandatory constraints usable with no Git repository or extension package. Do not remove advanced functionality to make the default smaller.\n\nExpose capability state and requirements through narrow shared SDK semantics, consumed by thin CLI/MCP adapters. Separate:\n- availability (what this runtime can provide);\n- configured policy (what the workspace enables/requires);\n- installation/operational state (what is actually ready);\n- applicable mandatory constraints (what the requested action must obey).\n\n### 2. Make Git integration an explicit optional adapter\n\nIntroduce a versioned persisted policy with `disabled | optional | required` semantics for Git/merge integration, independent of bundled packages and governance presets:\n- disabled: no automatic Git setup or missing-driver remediation; core remains usable;\n- optional: integration is discoverable, with explicit setup and clearly advisory absence;\n- required: missing/broken required integration produces an actionable failing verdict.\n\nTreat detection of a Git repository as availability, not user consent to mandatory installation. Document the chosen fresh-workspace default and an explicit opt-in route. Non-Git workspaces should not receive setup warnings for an unused integration.\n\nInspect [merge installation and package identity coupling](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/src/sdk/merge/install.ts#L392-L445), [validation](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/src/sdk/governance/validate.ts#L3252-L3304), and health together so all entry points share the same policy. Preserve genuine conflicts, pending decisions, and integrity failures regardless of whether future integration is enabled.\n\nProvide explicit status and reversible configuration transitions. Changing policy must not silently delete existing Git configuration, attributes, hooks, or receipts. If a supported removal operation is added, preview exactly the PM-owned entries, preserve unrelated/user-edited content, and document recovery.\n\n### 3. Separate health verdict from exit policy, with safe migration\n\nResolve capability policy first; calculate one authoritative health verdict; then map that verdict to exit status. `--strict-exit` should control how the verdict becomes an exit code, not silently turn an optional capability into a requirement. An explicit requirement remains authoritative.\n\nDo not weaken existing CI silently. Version the settings/contract change and document old-to-new behavior. Existing workspaces retain effective protections until an explicit migration; legacy strict-exit consumers need a compatibility path and a documented explicit required policy/flag. Define and test precedence across stored policy, explicit command options, and legacy compatibility before release. Surface unsupported/conflicting policy states with an actionable error.\n\n### 4. Make guidance, help and errors progressively discoverable\n\nGenerate a short domain-neutral quickstart for basic create/find/update/close. Add coding, claims, tests, merge and advanced execution guidance only when relevant to selected capabilities and configured policy.\n\nUse the existing type/command registry to discover actual available types, fields, custom commands and package capabilities dynamically. A compact first view must include clear routes to command-specific contracts, full help, schema/type discovery and customization. Preserve canonical aliases and machine-readable continuation/omission receipts, including extension-provided capabilities.\n\nKeep required constraints, active blockers, insufficient context, destructive effects and uncertainty visible at the decision point. “Compact” cannot hide policy, discard evidence required to act, or cause agents to mistake a partial result for a complete one. Optional detail can be fetched on demand with truthful summaries/counts and usable continuations.\n\nErrors should lead with the concrete problem and a valid recovery; reuse lean errors rather than adding a second format. Do not suggest fabricated fields/dependencies or bypassing configured mandatory rules.\n\n### 5. Measure total effort on real tasks\n\nExtend #1362 / the existing [task-token gate](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/1e7ecc1bc34fb496739c590ef2e45ca7dc45e21d/scripts/release/agent-task-token-gate.mjs). Run six realistic tasks × two approaches × three independent runs (36 trajectories), using the same model, provider, model settings, task inputs and equivalent starting evidence.\n\nCompare PM against a documented lightweight baseline of Markdown/JSON files plus `rg`. Use fresh independent workspaces and counterbalanced order. Initial tasks:\n1. Create, find, update and close a non-coding personal task.\n2. Orient in an existing project and select valid unblocked work.\n3. Record a decision, resume later and recover its relevant context.\n4. Update a dependency and choose the correct next action.\n5. Use a custom non-coding type/field discovered at runtime.\n6. Recover from an invalid command or incomplete read and finish safely.\n\nRecord provider-reported input/output tokens (and cached/reasoning counts where exposed), all tool/help/error/continuation exchanges, retries, commands, latency, correctness and terminal completion. Avoid double-counting tool text already included in provider input. Report unavailable metrics explicitly and keep bytes/4 estimates separately labeled. Score final artifacts/state against an independent oracle; no success credit for fabricated evidence, ignored blockers, or falsely claiming completion.\n\nReport each task and run, variability, correctness and total cost together. Include setup/discovery in cold-start costs and disclose any amortization separately. Establish the baseline before setting improvement targets; do not invent a percentage win. Keep deterministic safety tests as release gates even if live-model evaluation is optional.\n\n## Acceptance and test matrix\n\n- [ ] Fresh non-Git generic workflow succeeds without packages, claims, tests, Git warnings or coding-only instructions\n- [ ] Fresh Git workspace can use the same minimal core without silently installing/requiring optional integrations\n- [ ] Disabled / optional / required states are consistent across SDK, CLI, MCP, init, health and validation\n- [ ] Required integration failures remain failing; stricter exit handling does not independently change capability policy\n- [ ] Legacy migration is explicit, preserves configured safety and user-owned files, and documents recovery\n- [ ] Compact discovery reaches all installed/custom capabilities; full-detail and continuation paths are executable\n- [ ] Mandatory constraints, blockers and incomplete-context outcomes remain visible in compact output\n- [ ] Packed Node/Bun consumers and CLI/MCP parity cover minimal, extended, legacy, malformed-setting and custom-type fixtures\n- [ ] The 36-trajectory comparison reports total agent effort and correct outcomes, separately from scripted output-token estimates\n- [ ] Existing quality, security, coverage, completeness and token ratchets remain intact\n\n## Further audit findings\n\nReproduced on published pm 2026.9.30 using isolated synthetic trackers. Each issue includes reproduction, implementation plan and regression acceptance:\n- #1364 — Recovery drops tracker scope/format and can read the wrong project's item\n- #1365 — Structured refusal metadata identifies a valid argument instead of the actual invalid field\n- #1366 — Equivalent JSON selectors disagree on error formatting\n- #1367 — Handoff restoration instructions do not restore the advertised omitted context\n- #1368 — Context next silently accepts and ignores inherited projection/depth controls\n- #1369 — Claim-and-start violates the compact canonical mutation receipt contract\n- #1370 — Transaction dry-runs report success for semantically invalid plans on unchanged state\n- #1371 — Budget recovery poisons producer cursor identity, preventing complete pagination across list/search/context\n\nPrioritize scope correctness (#1364), complete reads (#1371), and truthful preflight (#1370), followed by coherent machine diagnostics and executable discovery/recovery. These are shared-contract fixes, not reasons to add default agent ceremony.\n\nImplement a cross-command conformance matrix over format aliases, success/error paths, tracker scope, projection and continuation composition, preview/apply parity, canonical mutation receipts and executable recovery. Reuse existing SDK/CLI contracts and tests instead of adding another output architecture.\n\nPassed controls from this audit include batch/completion rollback, transaction replay without duplicate evidence, reopen preserving unrelated metadata, ordinary producer-only pagination, filter behavior and explicit empty-result handling. These checks do not establish that the entire system is defect-free or that total agent tokens are lower. No live-model comparison or implementation fixes were performed.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNo implementation code in this issue; no removal of customization; no second accounting system; no universal coding workflow imposed on non-coding projects; no weakening of mandatory constraints to win a token benchmark."
